    "created_at": "2023-04-07T03:34:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-04-07T03:34:56Z",
    "body": "Sorry for the bump, but I think this issue got overlooked at some point.\r\n\r\n@t-mangoe , can we test this anywhere? I'd love to see this.\r\n\r\nI should mention, I'm only looking for this, because there are cases where the shell variable `$USER` is not set as root, causing `$user` in starship to just be blank when I'm root. The desire for a separate `format_root` is more of a workaround to that (I was literally going to hardcode `root` into the format string), but there are definitely many uses for this, like showing a `#` or a `$` depending on which is which.\r\n\r\nAnd since we're talking about conditional formatting, I would _love_ to be able to programmatically change all my colors for _all_ configurations/modules if I'm root. I like having everything red if I'm root, but to do that now I'm pretty sure you have to maintain two configs with different colors.\r\n\r\nMaybe both this issue, and that problem, could be addressed with a top-level method of substitution based on if you're root or not? That way you could just take the generated shell prompt and start replacing things, like turn a `$` into a `#`, or turn a color like `#0000FF` or `blue` into `red`.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
